Final C++ project: a **contact manager**. It combines a `struct` for data, a `std::vector` for storage, and STL algorithms for sorting — the same shape as real-world inventory and booking systems.

### struct Contact

A `struct` is a class whose members are `public` by default, ideal for plain data groups:

```cpp
struct Contact {
    std::string name;
    std::string phone;
};
```

### Storing contacts

```cpp
std::vector<Contact> contacts;
contacts.push_back({"Alice", "555-0101"});
contacts.push_back({"Bob", "555-0102"});
```

### Finding a contact

Search by scanning the vector and comparing names:

```cpp
std::string target = "Bob";
for (const Contact& c : contacts) {
    if (c.name == target) {
        std::cout << c.phone << std::endl;
    }
}
```

### Sorting by name

`std::sort` needs to know *how* to compare two `Contact` objects. Give it a lambda that compares `name`:

```cpp
std::sort(contacts.begin(), contacts.end(),
          [](const Contact& a, const Contact& b) {
              return a.name < b.name;
          });
```

After sorting, the vector is ordered alphabetically by name, ready to print or to binary-search.

### Putting it together

The pattern — **struct for data, vector for collection, algorithm for operation** — is how real C++ programs are built. Data stays grouped (name and phone travel together), the collection grows and shrinks safely, and the standard algorithms guarantee correct ordering. Extend it later with a phone lookup, a delete, or file persistence, and you've built a genuine application.

